"CSS Syntax Module Level 3 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-syntax-3/)\nciteturn1180view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn1179view0\",\"pattern\":\"If the next input code point is EOF\"}); Total lines: 2053\nL465: Otherwise, if the input stream cite193†starts with an ident sequence , cite198†reconsume the current input code point , cite201†consume an ident-like token , and return it.\nL466: \nL467: Otherwise, return a cite159†<delim-token> with its value set to the cite170†current input code point .\nL468: \nL469: U+002E FULL STOP (.)\nL470:     If the input stream cite197†starts with a number , cite198†reconsume the current input code point , cite199†consume a numeric token , and return it.\nL471: Otherwise, return a cite159†<delim-token> with its value set to the cite170†current input code point .\nL472: \nL473: U+003A COLON (:)\nL474:     Return a cite202†<colon-token> .\nL475: U+003B SEMICOLON (;)\nL476:     Return a cite130†<semicolon-token> .\nL477: U+003C LESS-THAN SIGN (<)\nL478:     If the cite171†next 3 input code points are U+0021 EXCLAMATION MARK U+002D HYPHEN-MINUS U+002D HYPHEN-MINUS (!--), consume them and return a cite167†<CDO-token> .\nL479: \nL480: Otherwise, return a cite159†<delim-token> with its value set to the cite170†current input code point .\nL481: U+0040 COMMERCIAL AT (@)\nL482:     If the cite171†next 3 input code points cite193†would start an ident sequence , cite194†consume an ident sequence , create an cite129†<at-keyword-token> with its value set to the returned value, and return it.\nL483: \nL484: Otherwise, return a cite159†<delim-token> with its value set to the cite170†current input code point .\nL485: U+005B LEFT SQUARE BRACKET ([)\nL486:     Return a cite203†<[-token> .\nL487: U+005C REVERSE SOLIDUS (\\)\nL488:     If the input stream cite192†starts with a valid escape , cite198†reconsume the current input code point , cite201†consume an ident-like token , and return it. Tests\nL489: \nL490:   * cite204†url-whitespace-consumption.html†wpt.fyi cite205†(live test)†wpt.live cite206†(source)†github.com L491: \nL492: Otherwise, this is a cite207†parse error . Return a cite159†<delim-token> with its value set to the cite170†current input code point .\nL493: U+005D RIGHT SQUARE BRACKET (])\nL494:     Return a cite208†<]-token> .\nL495: U+007B LEFT CURLY BRACKET ({)\nL496:     Return a cite131†<{-token> .\nL497: U+007D RIGHT CURLY BRACKET (})\nL498:     Return a cite132†<}-token> .\nL499: cite175†digit L500:     cite198†Reconsume the current input code point , cite199†consume a numeric token , and return it.\nL501: U+0055 LATIN CAPITAL LETTER U (U)\nL502: u+0075 LATIN LOWERCASE LETTER U (u)\nL504: Otherwise, cite198†reconsume the current input code point , cite201†consume an ident-like token , and return it.\nL505: \nL506: cite121†ident-start code point L507:     cite198†Reconsume the current input code point , cite201†consume an ident-like token , and return it.\nL508: EOF\nL509:     Return an cite153†<EOF-token> .\nL510: anything else\nL511:     Return a cite159†<delim-token> with its value set to the cite170†current input code point .\nL512: Tests\nL515: #### 4.3.2. Consume comments\nL516: \nL517: This section describes how to consume comments from a stream of cite104†code points†infra.spec.whatwg.org . It returns nothing.\nL518: \nL519: If the cite171†next two input code point are U+002F SOLIDUS (/) followed by a U+002A ASTERISK (*), consume them and all following cite104†code points†infra.spec.whatwg.org up to and including the first U+002A ASTERISK (*) followed by a U+002F SOLIDUS (/), or up to an EOF code point. Return to the start of this step.\nL520: If the preceding paragraph ended by consuming an EOF code point, this is a cite207†parse error .\nL521: \nL522: Return nothing.\nL523: #### 4.3.3. Consume a numeric token\nL524: \nL525: This section describes how to consume a numeric token from a stream of cite104†code points†infra.spec.whatwg.org . It returns either a cite160†<number-token> , cite161†<percentage-token> , or cite162†<dimension-token> .\nL526: \nL527: cite217†Consume a number and let `number` be the result.\nL528: \nL529: If the cite171†next 3 input code points cite193†would start an ident sequence , then:\nL530:   1. Create a cite162†<dimension-token> with the same value, type flag, and sign character as `number`, and a unit set initially to the empty string.\nL531:   2. cite194†Consume an ident sequence . Set the cite162†<dimension-token> ’s unit to the returned value.\nL532:   3. Return the cite162†<dimension-token> .\nL533: \nL534: Otherwise, if the cite171†next input code point is U+0025 PERCENTAGE SIGN (%), consume it. Create a cite161†<percentage-token> with the same value and sign character as `number`, and return it.\nL536: #### 4.3.4. Consume an ident-like token\nL537: \nL538: This section describes how to consume an ident-like token from a stream of cite104†code points†infra.spec.whatwg.org . It returns an cite154†<ident-token> , cite155†<function-token> , cite158†<url-token> , or cite218†<bad-url-token> .\nL539: \nL540: cite194†Consume an ident sequence , and let `string` be the result.\nL541: If `string`’s value is an cite219†ASCII case-insensitive†infra.spec.whatwg.org match for \"url\", and the cite171†next input code point is U+0028 LEFT PARENTHESIS ((), consume it. While the next two input code points are cite128†whitespace , consume the next input code point.\nL542: If the next one or two input code points are U+0022 QUOTATION MARK (\"), U+0027 APOSTROPHE ('), or whitespace followed by U+0022 QUOTATION MARK (\") or U+0027 APOSTROPHE ('), then create a cite155†<function-token> with its value set to `string` and return it. Otherwise, cite220†consume a url token , and return it.\nL543: Otherwise, if the cite171†next input code point is U+0028 LEFT PARENTHESIS ((), consume it. Create a cite155†<function-token> with its value set to `string` and return it.\nL544: \nL545: Otherwise, create an cite154†<ident-token> with its value set to `string` and return it.\nL546: #### 4.3.5. Consume a string token\nL547: \nL548: This section describes how to consume a string token from a stream of cite104†code points†infra.spec.whatwg.org . It returns either a cite157†<string-token> or cite221†<bad-string-token> .\nL549: \nL550: This algorithm may be called with an `ending code point`, which denotes the cite104†code point†infra.spec.whatwg.org that ends the string. If an `ending code point` is not specified, the cite170†current input code point is used.\nL551: Initially create a cite157†<string-token> with its value set to the empty string.\nL552: \nL553: Repeatedly consume the cite171†next input code point from the stream:\nL554: \nL555: `ending code point`\nL556:     Return the cite157†<string-token> .\nL557: EOF\nL558:     This is a cite207†parse error . Return the cite157†<string-token> .\nL559: cite127†newline L560:     This is a cite207†parse error . cite198†Reconsume the current input code point , create a cite221†<bad-string-token> , and return it.\nL561: U+005C REVERSE SOLIDUS (\\)\nL562:     If the cite171†next input code point is EOF, do nothing.\nL563: Otherwise, if the cite171†next input code point is a newline, consume it.\nL564: \nL565: Otherwise, (the stream cite192†starts with a valid escape ) cite222†consume an escaped code point and append the returned cite104†code point†infra.spec.whatwg.org to the cite157†<string-token> ’s value.\nL566: \nL567: anything else\nL568:     Append the cite170†current input code point to the cite157†<string-token> ’s value.\nL569: Tests\nL570: \nL571:   * cite214†escaped-eof.html†wpt.fyi cite215†(live test)†wpt.live cite216†(source)†github.com L572: #### 4.3.6. Consume a url token\nL573: \nL574: This section describes how to consume a url token from a stream of cite104†code points†infra.spec.whatwg.org . It returns either a cite158†<url-token> or a cite218†<bad-url-token> .\nL575: Note: This algorithm assumes that the initial \"url(\" has already been consumed. This algorithm also assumes that it’s being called to consume an \"unquoted\" value, like url(foo). A quoted value, like url(\"foo\"), is parsed as a cite155†<function-token> . cite201†Consume an ident-like token automatically handles this distinction; this algorithm shouldn’t be called directly otherwise.\nL576:   1. Initially create a cite158†<url-token> with its value set to the empty string.\nL577:   2. Consume as much cite128†whitespace as possible.\nL578:   3. Repeatedly consume the cite171†next input code point from the stream:\nL579: U+0029 RIGHT PARENTHESIS ())\nL580:     Return the cite158†<url-token> .\nL581: EOF\nL582:     This is a cite207†parse error . Return the cite158†<url-token> .\nL583: cite128†whitespace L584:     Consume as much cite128†whitespace as possible. If the cite171†next input code point is U+0029 RIGHT PARENTHESIS ()) or EOF, consume it and return the cite158†<url-token> (if EOF was encountered, this is a cite207†parse error ); otherwise, cite223†consume the remnants of a bad url , create a cite218†<bad-url-token> , and return it.\nL585: U+0022 QUOTATION MARK (\")\nL586: U+0027 APOSTROPHE (')\nL587: U+0028 LEFT PARENTHESIS (()\nL588: cite224†non-printable code point L589:     This is a cite207†parse error . cite223†Consume the remnants of a bad url , create a cite218†<bad-url-token> , and return it.\nL590: U+005C REVERSE SOLIDUS (\\)\nL591:     If the stream cite192†starts with a valid escape , cite222†consume an escaped code point and append the returned cite104†code point†infra.spec.whatwg.org to the cite158†<url-token> ’s value.\nL592: Otherwise, this is a cite207†parse error . cite223†Consume the remnants of a bad url , create a cite218†<bad-url-token> , and return it.\nL593: \nL594: anything else\nL595:     Append the cite170†current input code point to the cite158†<url-token> ’s value.\nL596: Tests\nL597: \nL598:   * cite225†unclosed-url-at-eof.html†wpt.fyi cite226†(live test)†wpt.live cite227†(source)†github.com L599: #### 4.3.7. Consume an escaped code point\nL600: \nL601: This section describes how to consume an escaped code point. It assumes that the U+005C REVERSE SOLIDUS (\\) has already been consumed and that the next input code point has already been verified to be part of a valid escape. It will return a cite104†code point†infra.spec.whatwg.org .\nL602: \nL603: Consume the cite171†next input code point .\nL605:     Consume as many cite126†hex digits as possible, but no more than 5. Note that this means 1-6 hex digits have been consumed in total. If the cite171†next input code point is cite128†whitespace , consume it as well. Interpret the hex digits as a hexadecimal number. If this number is zero, or is for a cite148†surrogate†infra.spec.whatwg.org , or is greater than the cite228†maximum allowed code point , return U+FFFD REPLACEMENT CHARACTER (�). Otherwise, return the cite104†code point†infra.spec.whatwg.org with that value.\nL606: EOF\nL607:     This is a cite207†parse error . Return U+FFFD REPLACEMENT CHARACTER (�).\nL608: anything else\nL609:     Return the cite170†current input code point .\nL610: Tests\nL612: #### 4.3.8. Check if two code points are a valid escape\nL613: \nL614: This section describes how to check if two code points are a valid escape. The algorithm described here can be called explicitly with two cite104†code points†infra.spec.whatwg.org , or can be called with the input stream itself. In the latter case, the two code points in question are the cite170†current input code point and the cite171†next input code point , in that order.\nL615: Note: This algorithm will not consume any additional cite104†code point†infra.spec.whatwg.org .\nL616: \nL617: If the first cite104†code point†infra.spec.whatwg.org is not U+005C REVERSE SOLIDUS (\\), return false.\nL618: \nL619: Otherwise, if the second cite104†code point†infra.spec.whatwg.org is a cite127†newline , return false.\nL620: \nL621: Otherwise, return true.\nL622: #### 4.3.9. Check if three code points would start an ident sequence\nL623: \nL624: This section describes how to check if three code points would start an cite120†ident sequence . The algorithm described here can be called explicitly with three cite104†code points†infra.spec.whatwg.org , or can be called with the input stream itself. In the latter case, the three code points in question are the cite170†current input code point and the cite171†next two input code points , in that order.\nL641: Note: This algorithm will not consume any additional cite104†code points†infra.spec.whatwg.org .\nL642: \nL643: Look at the first cite104†code point†infra.spec.whatwg.org :\nL644: \nL645: U+002B PLUS SIGN (+)\nL694:   1. Let `type` be the string \"integer\". Let `number part` and `exponent part` be the empty string.\nL695:   2. If the cite171†next input code point is U+002B PLUS SIGN (+) or U+002D HYPHEN-MINUS (-), consume it. Append it to `number part` and set `sign character` to it.\nL696:   3. While the cite171†next input code point is a cite175†digit , consume it and append it to `number part`.\nL697:   4. If the cite171†next 2 input code points are U+002E FULL STOP (.) followed by a cite175†digit , then:\nL698:     1. Consume the cite171†next input code point and append it to `number part`.\nL699:     2. While the cite171†next input code point is a cite175†digit , consume it and append it to `number part`.\nL700:     3. Set `type` to \"number\".\nL701:   5. If the cite171†next 2 or 3 input code points are U+0045 LATIN CAPITAL LETTER E (E) or U+0065 LATIN SMALL LETTER E (e), optionally followed by U+002D HYPHEN-MINUS (-) or U+002B PLUS SIGN (+), followed by a cite175†digit , then:\nL702:     1. Consume the cite171†next input code point .\n"
